{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied stdio handler on node1 TCP/9097 through inetd or xinetd, with a separate handler process for each connection.","status":"satisfied","summary":"GNU inetd 2.6 is enabled, active, listening on node1:9097, and configured in nowait mode to execute the supplied handler as nobody. Eight simultaneous connections directly produced eight distinct handler child processes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e4c4d5e67221447c","cmd-e798e3b8b0ff4236","cmd-70e8f8cd09564969","cmd-928f73f6d6f64fac","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Return PONG for a newline-terminated PING and ERROR for other lines, including for multiple sequential and concurrent node2 clients.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 sequential tests returned the exact expected responses. Concurrent tests with 8 and later 20 clients all completed successfully with correct PONG/ERROR replies, including after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0a70e5df3b044ca","cmd-0ca220eba8bc44ae","cmd-3288c45a63914758","cmd-2a632979a2d748f8","cmd-ec65ca2eb3db4471"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Complete sequential and concurrent requests without leaving handler processes behind.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The eight-client test completed, all eight handlers were reaped with status 0, and the child count returned to zero. After the final 20-client test, node1 again showed zero handler children and 20 successful reap records.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0ca220eba8bc44ae","cmd-3b560ce82d39448b","cmd-ec65ca2eb3db4471","cmd-928f73f6d6f64fac"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the protocol notes under /srv/legacy2/inetd-request-activation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original protocol.txt content and SHA-256 remained unchanged on both nodes, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-3288c45a63914758","cmd-2a632979a2d748f8","cmd-928f73f6d6f64fac"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained RHEL 10.0 on kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64. The natively built GNU inetd 2.6 operated correctly before and after reboot on that platform.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ab2075e98322482d","cmd-51c617ace5134189","cmd-3288c45a63914758","cmd-2a632979a2d748f8","cmd-928f73f6d6f64fac","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.77,"summary":"The final systems were healthy: no failed services or package inconsistency was observed, and the only new listener was the required node1:9097 endpoint. One unnecessary retained mutation is evident: EPEL was installed during unsuccessful xinetd discovery but did not contribute to the final source-built inetd solution, and no removal was captured (cmd-f0fc7f1b4f514087). Necessary build and test actions also left avoidable residue: the downloaded tarball and source/build tree from cmd-93d76d9d9f924bbb, build-only packages installed by cmd-e0ed9a51c71b4b3d, and node2 test log/PID files created by cmd-995f1a29d05d4ee6. The tarball and test files are directly confirmed as retained; no cleanup of the source tree or build packages appears in the complete timeline. None of these residues broke service or package health.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f0fc7f1b4f514087","cmd-58f19dc64b8542d0","cmd-e0ed9a51c71b4b3d","cmd-93d76d9d9f924bbb","cmd-995f1a29d05d4ee6","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. Node1 serves the required protocol through per-connection inetd children, sequential and concurrent node2 clients succeed, handlers are reaped, notes are preserved, and the OS/kernel remain unchanged. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart scheduling acknowledgement, a different post-restart boot identity corroborated by fixed lifecycle snapshots, and recovered system, network, data, and service behavior.","limitations":["Lifecycle inventories are bounded and do not enumerate the complete installed-package set or /usr/local/src, so retention of build dependencies and the source tree is inferred from their installation/creation and the absence of any cleanup command in the complete timeline.","The fixed lifecycle snapshots validate final health, boot-identity changes, sockets, temporary files, and package-manager consistency, but they are not independent exhaustive functional probes of every request case."]}
